<h3 id="expected-approach-1-using-two-pointers-on-time-and-o1-space"><span>[Expected Approach &#8211; 1] Using Two Pointers &#8211; O(n) Time and O(1) Space</span></h3><blockquote><p dir="ltr"><span>The idea is to maintain two pointers: </span><b><strong>left </strong></b><span>and </span><b><strong>right</strong></b><span>, such that </span><b><strong>left </strong></b><span>points at the </span><b><strong>beginning </strong></b><span>of the array and </span><b><strong>right </strong></b><span>points to the </span><b><strong>end </strong></b><span>of the array. </span></p>
<p dir="ltr"><span>While left pointer is less than the right pointer, swap the elements at these two positions. After each swap, </span><b><strong>increment </strong></b><span>the </span><b><strong>left </strong></b><span>pointer and </span><b><strong>decrement </strong></b><span>the </span><b><strong>right </strong></b><span>pointer to move towards the center of array. This will swap all the elements in the first half with their corresponding element in the second half.</span></p>
